CONTEXT MANAGEMENT

Use `manage_context` after substantial investigation, resolved conflicts, failed
approaches, or large obsolete file or log output when you no longer need the
older detail verbatim. Do not compact mechanically on every turn. Make at most
one compaction request in a turn, and never pair it with `final_answer`.

The tool description specifies how to schedule compaction. When parallel calls
are supported, pair compaction with the next independent useful tool. When calls
are sequential, call compaction alone and perform the next useful operation in
the following turn. Results from a companion tool remain available after
compaction. Keep dependent operations sequential, including cherry-picks,
changes to the same file or repository, patch regeneration, `%prep`, SRPM
creation, and builds.

Normally use `keep_recent_exchanges=1`; increase it only when more recent
evidence must remain verbatim. The current exchange counts toward this number.
The `durable_summary` must be a COMPLETE, CUMULATIVE DURABLE SUMMARY. Each new
summary replaces the old summary rather than being merged with it. Summarize
observed results only and retain:

- the chosen dist-git, spec-only, cherry-pick, or git-am approach; actual target,
  unpacked-source, upstream, and source-dist-git paths; branch and base commit;
- ordered source commit and patch identities, completed and remaining work,
  prerequisites, active cherry-pick or git-am state, conflicts, and resolutions;
- generated patch paths and source mapping, Patch tag and strip-level decisions,
  and currently permitted spec changes;
- brief references to relevant fetched rules and unresolved retrieval or rule
  conflicts; fetched rule exchanges are retained verbatim by the runner;
- latest relevant `%prep`, SRPM, build, and test results, artifact and log paths,
  and build identifiers, clearly separating completed and pending checks;
- every failed approach, why it failed, what must not be repeated, the current
  hypothesis, and the next useful step.

Do not repeat the full task or system instructions. The package, target branch,
Jira and CVE identifiers, original patch list, and triage instructions remain in
protected task messages.

Rule-fetch exchanges remain intact and take precedence over an agent-written
summary. Refer to them for patch conventions and spec permissions. A missing or
failed retrieval grants no permission. Re-fetch when rules may be stale,
uncertain, or conflicting; retained old evidence does not prove it is current.
Core workflow instructions still take precedence, and package rules take
precedence over conflicting shared rules.

A normal full retry keeps conversation history while resetting the checkout. A
later reset task supersedes repository progress in an older summary: inspect the
current files and Git state again while retaining lessons from failed work.
Incremental repairs use a fresh agent. Continue reading and updating the
`fix-attempts.md` file at the diagnostics path in the repair task. In-memory
summaries do not replace that file; logs and notes stay in the sibling
`<local_clone>-build-logs` directory outside both Git worktrees.
